The security department is recommending the School Board renew a contract on Wednesday with D-Tek Enterprises, Inc., to provide five Labrador retreivers to sniff out drugs in school lockers and student cars and give educational program.
"We feel it's been a very efficient program in two areas -- No. 1, in education, especially at the junior high and elementary level. The interest of the students is at a high level," said Ken Schrimsher, associate superintendent for planning and operations.
"The second thing is it really deters students from bringing drugs on campus. "
District officials would like to expand the program from five to seven dogs, but because of budget constraints, will have to delay any increases, Schrimsher said.
Of the district's 101 schools, 27 are participating in the program this year.
The cost to renew the program is $205,418.75 for three years.
Officials warn that they don't think drug abuse among students is eliminated -- it is just kept off campus.
The program also relieves peer pressure for some students, said Martha Jones, security operations director.
"That gives them an out," she said. "They can say 'I don't want to use it because the dog might come today.' I do think it's a good program."
Since the dogs are amiable Labrador retreivers instead of the more intimidating German shepherds, many students feel affection for the dogs, Jones said.
"They're not threatening to the normal student -- once they get used to them, it's nice to see a pretty dog," she said, recounting how North Shore High School students sent cards to one injured dog. "The majority of kids adopt the dogs as pets or mascots."
The program is open to all Palm Beach County high school students, regardless of age, race, gender or sexual orientation, according to the district.
For more information about the program, visit the district's website at www.pflb.org/drug-sniffing-program.
