Sunglasses and shirtsleeves have been the racegoers' uniform at several recent Cheltenham Festivals, but this year we need no reminding that jumping is the winter code. Officials at the course emerged from hiding yesterday morning to find that the track had turned its face to the gales on Sunday night and survived relatively unscathed. Daytime temperatures, though, are unlikely to reach double figures any time soon. It promises to be a hipflask Festival.
If that feels a little old-fashioned, a throwback to the days when even some of the jockeys took a slug before a big race, then so much the better. This is a Festival that will be defined - for better or for worse - by a single race, the Gold Cup that National Hunt has been anticipating for the last 12 months and, in a sense, for 44 years in all. The last time Cheltenham staged a race like the one between Kauto Star and Denman, it was in black-and-white.
Anyone who had a bet - a legal one, at any rate - on the last great Gold Cup head-to-head between Arkle and Mill House in 1964 is now at least 62 years old. We might easily be waiting another lifetime for the next one, so it is hardly surprising that it has dominated all thoughts in recent weeks. Opinions and loyalties have been forged over the course of a year or more, and it is now just three days until those in the opposite camp are made to pay for their foolishness.
The danger, of course, is that reality will not match expectation. An early mishap for either horse could bleed away much of the excitement before they have set out of the final circuit, so it would make sense to bank some memories beforehand, just in case.
Sizing Europe's performance in the Champion Hurdle this afternoon is an obvious place to start, if only because he already looks like a top chaser for two or three years' time. You can see it in his frame and the way he arches over his hurdles. If he has the class to be a champion over timber first, the Irish in particular will leave with something to look forward to, next season and beyond.
Or perhaps it will be Harchibald who finally comes good on the hill, three years after he jumped the last just behind Hardy Eustace with Paul Carberry taking a pull. Carberry was probably the only jockey alive with the sheer nerve to wait until a few strides from the line on a horse who appeared to have so much left to give. He is certainly the only one with enough cheery mischief in him to try it again.
